course: dictionary
lesson: 11508
title: Doing things aimlessly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An adverb tells you how something is done. Aimlessly tells you how someone moves or acts: with no purpose, plan or direction. «They wandered aimlessly round town.» They were not going to the shops or to school. They were just walking, with no goal in mind.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. Words like wandered, drifted and kicked show movement with no clear target. «The boat drifted aimlessly down the river.» A drifting boat has no captain steering it to a harbour, so it goes wherever the water takes it.
explain: Aimless is the root word, and it means having no aim. Adding -ly turns it into an adverb, so aimlessly describes an action. Ask yourself: is someone going somewhere on purpose, or just drifting? If the answer is drifting, aimlessly fits.
fact: Aimlessly is an adverb, and it means without any purpose, plan or direction.
fact: «Aimlessly» often describes wandering, drifting or moving about with no goal.
fact: «Alice wandered aimlessly through the wood.» She had no plan and no path to follow.
fact: Aim means a target or a goal, so aimless means having no target or goal.
fact: The opposite idea is purposefully: doing something with a clear plan and goal.
exercise: «They wandered aimlessly round town all afternoon.» What does aimlessly mean? | In a bright and happy way | Very quickly and loudly | With a clear plan to visit every shop | *With no purpose or direction
exercise: «The boat drifted aimlessly down the river.» What is the boat doing? | Sinking slowly | *Floating along with no goal or steering | Stopping at the harbour | Racing to the sea
exercise: «He kicked the ball aimlessly against the wall, with no game to play.» Find and copy one word that is an adverb ending in -ly. | kicked | against | *aimlessly | game
test: «She walked aimlessly along the beach, not looking for shells or anything.» Which word tells you she had no goal? | shells | beach | walked | *aimlessly
test: Ravi says, «I went aimlessly to the park, but I wanted to meet Sam there.» Is this a good use of aimlessly? | Yes, because he went to the park | No, because Sam is not a word | *No, because he had a clear purpose: to meet Sam | Yes, because parks are big
exam: Which sentence uses aimlessly correctly? | She aimlessly finished her homework with a clear plan. | *The lost dog ran aimlessly around the field, not knowing where to go. | The team aimlessly planned their winning goal before the match. | He aimlessly built a model boat from a detailed set of instructions.
